// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["x-b"] = {"qs": [["Risk Management & Insurance Concepts", "Which risk is generally considered insurable?", ["Loss from a fall in share prices", "Accidental fire damage to a factory", "Loss due to a business decision going wrong", "A certain loss that will definitely happen"], 1, "Insurable risks are pure, accidental, measurable and affect many similar exposures. Speculative risks like share price falls and certain losses are not insurable."], ["Insurance Needs Analysis", "Why does a young earner's insurance need usually fall as he approaches retirement?", ["Insurance premiums become tax-free after 50", "Liabilities always increase with age", "Life insurance cannot be bought after 50", "Fewer years of income remain to protect"], 3, "Insurance replaces future income; as remaining working years shrink and assets grow, the gap insurance must fill gets smaller. Liabilities usually fall with age."], ["Health & General Insurance", "A personal accident policy typically covers:", ["Hospital bills for any illness", "Damage to the insured's car", "Accidental death and disability", "Theft of household goods"], 2, "Personal accident covers pay for death and permanent or temporary disability caused by accidents. Illness, motor damage and burglary need other covers."], ["Life Insurance Products", "Which life insurance policy provides cover for the whole of the insured's life rather than a fixed term?", ["Term insurance policy", "Money-back policy", "Whole life policy", "Endowment policy of 15 years"], 2, "A whole life policy covers the insured for life (often up to age 99 or 100), paying on death whenever it occurs. Term, money-back and endowment plans have fixed terms."], ["Risk Management & Insurance Concepts", "Pooling of risk in insurance works because:", ["Every policyholder is guaranteed to make a claim", "The insurer invests only in fixed deposits", "Losses of a few are paid from many premiums", "Premiums are refunded if no claim is made"], 2, "Insurance pools premiums from many people facing similar risks; only some suffer losses, which are paid from the common fund. The law of large numbers makes losses predictable."], ["Health & General Insurance", "Under IRDAI's rules, the maximum waiting period that insurers can apply for pre-existing diseases in health policies is:", ["3 years", "8 years", "1 month", "10 years"], 0, "IRDAI's 2024 health insurance norms cap the waiting period for pre-existing diseases at three years. This replaced the earlier maximum of four years."], ["Retirement Planning Concepts", "The accumulation phase of retirement planning refers to:", ["Years after retirement when the corpus is spent", "The month of retirement only", "Working years when the corpus is built", "The period after the corpus is exhausted"], 2, "During the accumulation phase, the client saves and invests to build the retirement corpus. The distribution phase starts at retirement, when the corpus funds expenses."], ["Wills, Succession & Trusts", "Which court document is generally needed by heirs to collect debts and securities of a deceased person who left no will?", ["Probate", "Codicil", "Power of attorney", "Succession certificate"], 3, "A succession certificate authorises heirs to collect debts and securities of an intestate deceased. Probate applies to a will, and a power of attorney ends at death."], ["Taxation of Investment Products", "Dividends received by a resident individual from Indian companies are:", ["Fully exempt", "Taxed at 10% flat", "Taxable at slab rates", "Taxed only in the company's hands"], 2, "Since the abolition of dividend distribution tax from April 2020, dividends are taxable in the hands of shareholders at their slab rates, with TDS above a threshold."], ["Income Tax Fundamentals", "A resident salaried individual opts for the new regime for FY 2025-26. Gross salary is ₹12,50,000 with no other income. After the standard deduction and rebate, his income tax is:", ["₹60,000", "Nil", "₹71,500", "₹1,25,000"], 1, "Taxable income = 12,50,000 − 75,000 standard deduction = ₹11,75,000, which is within the ₹12 lakh rebate limit for FY 2025-26, so tax becomes nil. ₹60,000 is the tax before rebate on ₹12 lakh."], ["Wealth Management & Estate Planning Concepts", "Which document lets a person appoint someone to manage his financial affairs while he is alive?", ["Will", "Probate", "Power of attorney", "Succession certificate"], 2, "A power of attorney authorises an agent to act on the principal's behalf during his lifetime. A will operates only after death, and probate and succession certificates are court documents."], ["Income Tax Fundamentals", "Rent received from a house property let out by an individual is taxed under the head:", ["Income from other sources", "Profits and gains of business", "Income from house property", "Capital gains"], 2, "Rental income from buildings and land appurtenant is taxed as income from house property, with a standard deduction allowed on net annual value. It is not business income unless property letting is the business itself."], ["Retirement Corpus & Distribution Planning", "A systematic withdrawal plan (SWP) from a mutual fund helps a retiree by:", ["Guaranteeing a fixed return for life", "Providing regular cash flow while staying invested", "Avoiding all taxes on withdrawals", "Locking money in for ten years"], 1, "An SWP redeems a set amount at intervals, offering regular income while the rest stays invested. Returns are not guaranteed, and each redemption may attract capital gains tax."], ["Life Insurance Products", "In a ULIP, the investment risk on the fund portion is borne by:", ["The policyholder", "The insurance company", "IRDAI", "The fund manager personally"], 0, "ULIP premiums, after charges, buy units whose value depends on market performance. The policyholder bears the investment risk, unlike guaranteed traditional plans."], ["Taxation of Investment Products", "Which investment gives tax-free interest and maturity proceeds for an individual?", ["Public Provident Fund", "Bank recurring deposit", "Corporate non-convertible debentures", "Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme"], 0, "PPF is in the exempt-exempt-exempt category: interest and maturity amounts are tax-free. Interest on RDs, NCDs and SCSS is taxable at slab rates."], ["Risk Management & Insurance Concepts", "Which statement about utmost good faith is correct?", ["Only the insurer must disclose facts", "Both proposer and insurer must disclose material facts", "Only facts asked in the form need disclosure", "It applies only to motor insurance"], 1, "Utmost good faith requires full disclosure of all material facts by both parties. Non-disclosure by the proposer can make the policy voidable, and insurers must also explain terms fairly."], ["Retirement Corpus & Distribution Planning", "A retiree withdraws 10% of his corpus each year while it earns 7%. What is the main risk?", ["The corpus will grow without limit", "His tax liability will fall to zero", "Inflation will be eliminated", "The corpus may run out early"], 3, "Withdrawing more than the corpus earns, especially after inflation, depletes capital each year and can exhaust it well before death. A sustainable withdrawal rate is needed."], ["Retirement Products & Pension Schemes", "Why might a retiree choose a 'joint life with return of purchase price' annuity?", ["Highest possible annuity rate for one person", "No income until both spouses die", "Guaranteed equity-linked returns", "Income for both spouses, capital to heirs"], 3, "This option pays income while either spouse lives and returns the purchase price to nominees after both die. The trade-off is a lower annuity rate than a single-life option."], ["Comprehensive Financial Planning & Case Studies", "Which action best shows client-centred advice in a case where the client is risk-averse but needs higher returns for a goal?", ["Push him into high-risk products without discussion", "Promise him high returns with low risk", "Ignore the goal and plan only for tax", "Discuss trade-offs like saving more or delaying"], 3, "When risk tolerance and required return conflict, the adviser should explain options such as increasing savings, extending the timeline or revising the goal, rather than ignoring the conflict."], ["Retirement Planning Concepts", "Which retirement risk relates to rising medical costs with age?", ["Reinvestment risk", "Healthcare cost risk", "Currency risk", "Credit risk"], 1, "Healthcare costs tend to rise faster than general inflation and increase with age, so retirement plans should include adequate health cover and a medical reserve."], ["Wealth Management & Estate Planning Concepts", "Which is a common objective of wealth management for an HNI family?", ["Avoiding all forms of investment risk", "Holding only cash in savings accounts", "Maximising the number of bank accounts", "Preserving and transferring wealth across generations"], 3, "Wealth management integrates investment, tax, risk and estate planning to grow, protect and pass on wealth. Avoiding all risk or holding only cash would erode real value."], ["Risk Management & Insurance Concepts", "A house worth ₹50 lakh is insured with two insurers for ₹50 lakh each. A fire causes ₹10 lakh of damage. Under the principle of contribution, the owner can recover:", ["₹10 lakh in total, shared by insurers", "₹20 lakh, ₹10 lakh from each insurer", "₹10 lakh from each insurer separately", "Nothing, because double insurance is illegal"], 0, "Contribution ensures the insured recovers only the actual loss, with insurers sharing it rateably. Indemnity prevents profit from insurance, so ₹20 lakh cannot be recovered."], ["Retirement Corpus & Distribution Planning", "A client wants ₹50 lakh in 15 years through a monthly SIP. Assuming 12% a year (1% a month), the monthly SIP needed is closest to:", ["₹27,778", "₹8,200", "₹10,008", "₹12,500"], 2, "Monthly SIP = FV × r ÷ ((1 + r)^n − 1) = 50,00,000 × 0.01 ÷ (1.01^180 − 1) ≈ ₹10,008. ₹27,778 simply divides the target by 180 months and ignores returns."], ["Retirement Planning Concepts", "Why does starting retirement savings ten years earlier usually reduce the monthly saving required?", ["Inflation stops affecting long-term savings", "Retirement expenses fall when one starts early", "Government matches all early contributions", "Money compounds for longer"], 3, "A longer accumulation period lets returns compound for more years, so each rupee saved early grows more. Inflation and expenses are unaffected by when saving begins."], ["Risk Management & Insurance Concepts", "A client keeps a large emergency fund to meet small medical bills himself instead of buying a low-deductible policy. This method of handling risk is:", ["Risk avoidance", "Risk transfer", "Risk elimination", "Risk retention"], 3, "Bearing small, predictable losses from one's own resources is risk retention. Insurance would be risk transfer, while avoidance means not undertaking the risky activity at all."], ["Comprehensive Financial Planning & Case Studies", "A client's education goal costs ₹15 lakh today and is 10 years away. At 8% education inflation, the future cost is closest to:", ["₹32.4 lakh", "₹27.0 lakh", "₹15.0 lakh", "₹40.0 lakh"], 0, "Future cost = 15 lakh × 1.08^10 ≈ ₹32.4 lakh. ₹27.0 lakh uses simple growth of 8% a year, which understates compounding inflation."], ["Retirement Products & Pension Schemes", "An NPS subscriber can choose 'auto choice'. This means:", ["All money is invested only in equity", "Asset mix changes with age", "The subscriber picks stocks personally", "Contributions are kept in a savings account"], 1, "Under auto choice, NPS assets are allocated across asset classes according to a life-cycle fund, reducing equity exposure as the subscriber ages. Active choice lets the subscriber set the mix."], ["Retirement Planning Concepts", "Expenses that typically fall after retirement include:", ["Commuting and work-related costs", "Medical and health insurance costs", "Leisure travel in early retirement", "Domestic help as mobility reduces"], 0, "Work-related costs such as commuting and work clothing usually fall after retirement, while health, leisure and assistance costs often rise."], ["Comprehensive Financial Planning & Case Studies", "A 35-year-old client has no life insurance, ₹5 lakh credit card debt and plans to start equity SIPs. What should come first?", ["Starting equity SIPs of the full surplus", "Buying a second home with a loan", "Investing in a new fund offer", "Clearing the card debt and buying term cover"], 3, "Protecting dependants and eliminating expensive debt come before long-term investing. Card interest far exceeds expected investment returns, and lack of cover exposes the family."], ["Insurance Needs Analysis", "Which is the most appropriate first step when reviewing an existing client's life cover?", ["Surrender all existing policies immediately", "Compare current cover with updated needs", "Buy an additional endowment plan", "Ignore cover once a policy is bought"], 1, "A review recalculates needs considering changes in income, family, loans and assets, then compares them with existing cover to identify gaps. Surrendering policies hastily can be costly."], ["Comprehensive Financial Planning & Case Studies", "A client needs ₹30 lakh for a goal in 4 years. Which allocation is most suitable?", ["Entirely in small-cap equity funds", "Entirely in a sector fund", "Mostly debt with limited equity", "Entirely in cryptocurrency"], 2, "Short horizons leave little time to recover from equity falls, so most of the money should be in debt, with only limited equity exposure if any."], ["Health & General Insurance", "A top-up policy has a deductible of ₹5 lakh. A single hospital bill is ₹7 lakh. Ignoring other limits, how much will the top-up insurer pay?", ["₹7 lakh", "₹5 lakh", "₹2 lakh", "Nothing"], 2, "A top-up pays only the amount above the deductible for a single claim: 7 lakh − 5 lakh = ₹2 lakh. The first ₹5 lakh must be met by the base policy or the insured."], ["Income Tax Fundamentals", "A father transfers money to his minor son, who earns interest on it. In whose hands is this interest usually taxed?", ["The parent with higher income", "The minor son alone", "Neither, as minors are exempt", "The bank paying the interest"], 0, "Income of a minor child is generally clubbed with the income of the parent whose income is higher, subject to small exemptions and exceptions such as income from the child's own skill."], ["Comprehensive Financial Planning & Case Studies", "A client in the highest tax bracket asks for a debt option. Which factor is most relevant to compare?", ["Pre-tax returns only", "Post-tax returns", "The colour of the brochure", "The number of branches of the issuer"], 1, "For high-bracket clients, tax treatment can change rankings significantly, so comparison should be on post-tax, risk-adjusted returns."], ["Risk Management & Insurance Concepts", "After paying a car damage claim, an insurer recovers the amount from the third party who caused the accident. This is based on the principle of:", ["Contribution", "Utmost good faith", "Subrogation", "Insurable interest"], 2, "Subrogation lets the insurer, after paying a claim, step into the insured's shoes to recover from the party responsible. It prevents the insured from being paid twice."], ["Wealth Management & Estate Planning Concepts", "A gift of money from a father to his daughter is:", ["Taxable as her income above ₹50,000", "Exempt in her hands, as she is a relative", "Taxable in the father's hands at 30%", "Prohibited under Indian law"], 1, "Gifts received from specified relatives, including parents, are not taxable in the recipient's hands. However, income later earned on certain gifts may be clubbed in specific cases, such as gifts to a spouse."], ["Wealth Management & Estate Planning Concepts", "Under the Companies Act, a nominee of shares receives the shares on the holder's death. According to the Supreme Court, the nominee:", ["Becomes the absolute owner in all cases", "Must sell them within one year", "Has no right to receive them", "Holds them for the legal heirs"], 3, "The Supreme Court has held that nomination does not override succession laws; the nominee receives the securities but holds them for the legal heirs or will beneficiaries."], ["Wealth Management & Estate Planning Concepts", "A client holds a bank account jointly with 'either or survivor' mandate. On his death, the bank will:", ["Freeze the account until a probate is obtained", "Pay the surviving joint holder", "Transfer the balance to the government", "Pay only the legal heirs named in a will"], 1, "With an either-or-survivor mandate, the survivor can operate the account and receive the balance. The survivor may still hold it for heirs under succession law."], ["Insurance Needs Analysis", "Disability income insurance is meant to:", ["Pay for vehicle repairs after an accident", "Repay home loans on job loss", "Replace income lost due to disability", "Fund children's education on death"], 2, "Disability income cover pays a portion of income if illness or injury prevents the insured from working. It addresses a risk often more likely than early death."], ["Income Tax Fundamentals", "Which of the following is a capital asset under income tax law?", ["Listed shares held as investment", "Stock-in-trade of a trader", "Personal clothing worn by the owner", "Agricultural land in a rural area"], 0, "Investments such as listed shares are capital assets. Stock-in-trade, personal effects like clothing and specified rural agricultural land are excluded from the definition."], ["Wills, Succession & Trusts", "Is registration of a will compulsory in India?", ["Yes, an unregistered will is void", "Only for wills of senior citizens", "Only if the estate exceeds ₹1 crore", "No, it is optional"], 3, "Registration of a will is optional; an unregistered will is valid if properly executed and attested. Registration can, however, help prove authenticity."], ["Retirement Planning Concepts", "Why should retirement planning assume a life expectancy beyond the average?", ["Average life expectancy is fixed by law", "Many people outlive the average", "Insurers require it for every client", "Corpus need falls with longer life"], 1, "Average life expectancy means about half of people live longer. Planning to an older age protects against outliving savings, the core of longevity risk."], ["Life Insurance Products", "A 30-year-old wants ₹1 crore of cover for 30 years at the lowest cost. Which product best meets this need?", ["Endowment plan", "Whole life plan with bonuses", "Money-back plan", "Term insurance plan"], 3, "Term insurance provides pure protection with no savings element, so premiums are far lower for the same cover. Endowment and money-back plans are much costlier per rupee of cover."], ["Wills, Succession & Trusts", "A will is automatically revoked when:", ["The executor dies before the testator", "The testator makes a later will revoking it", "A witness to the will changes address", "The testator's property rises in value"], 1, "A will can be revoked by a later will or codicil, or by destroying it with intent to revoke. The executor's death or a witness moving does not revoke it."], ["Wills, Succession & Trusts", "A 'living trust' created by a client during his lifetime can help by:", ["Avoiding probate and managing assets", "Increasing his income tax rate", "Cancelling all his nominations", "Making his assets public records"], 0, "A trust holds assets during life and after death per the deed, so the assets pass without probate and can be managed continuously. It also offers privacy."], ["Retirement Corpus & Distribution Planning", "A retiree buys an annuity for ₹50 lakh at an annuity rate of 7% a year. Her annual annuity income is:", ["₹3,50,000", "₹7,00,000", "₹35,000", "₹5,00,000"], 0, "Annual annuity = purchase price × annuity rate = 50,00,000 × 7% = ₹3,50,000. This income is generally fixed and taxable as per slab, so it loses purchasing power over time."], ["Taxation of Investment Products", "Interest on a bank fixed deposit is taxed for an individual:", ["At a flat 10% as capital gains", "At slab rates under other sources", "Only when the deposit matures", "Not at all if below ₹10 lakh"], 1, "FD interest is income from other sources taxed at slab rates, usually on an accrual basis each year. Banks deduct TDS above thresholds, but TDS is not the final tax."], ["Insurance Needs Analysis", "A client has ₹20 lakh of group health cover from his employer. What is the main risk of relying on it alone?", ["Cover may end when he leaves the job", "Group cover is never valid in hospitals", "Group cover always excludes his spouse", "Group cover can only be used outside India"], 0, "Employer cover usually lapses on leaving the job or retiring, when buying fresh cover may be harder or costlier. A personal policy provides continuity."], ["Insurance Needs Analysis", "In a needs-based analysis, which item reduces the additional life cover required?", ["Outstanding home loan", "Existing liquid investments and cover", "Children's future education costs", "Family's future living expenses"], 1, "Required cover = liabilities + future needs − existing assets and insurance. Loans, education costs and expenses add to the need; existing resources reduce it."], ["Retirement Planning Concepts", "A retiree's corpus earns 7% while inflation is 7%. Her real rate of return is:", ["Zero", "7%", "14%", "Negative 7%"], 0, "Real return = (1.07 ÷ 1.07) − 1 = 0. The corpus grows only enough to keep pace with inflation, so withdrawals in real terms simply deplete principal."], ["Income Tax Fundamentals", "Under the old regime, a deduction for life insurance premium and PPF contributions is available up to an overall limit for:", ["Any expense on consumer goods", "Specified savings and payments", "Rent paid to parents only", "Losses from share trading"], 1, "The old regime allows a deduction for specified investments and payments such as PPF, ELSS, life premiums and principal repayment of home loans, up to an overall ceiling. It is not available in the new regime."], ["Retirement Corpus & Distribution Planning", "Sequence-of-returns risk is highest:", ["At the start of a career", "When the corpus is very small", "Just before and after retirement", "Only for clients with annuities"], 2, "Poor returns soon after withdrawals begin can permanently deplete the corpus, since units are sold at low prices. This makes the years around retirement the most vulnerable."], ["Retirement Planning Concepts", "A client's current expenses are ₹30,000 a month and she retires in 25 years. At 6% inflation, her first-month retirement expense is closest to:", ["₹75,000", "₹1,05,000", "₹1,28,756", "₹1,80,000"], 2, "Future expense = 30,000 × 1.06^25 ≈ ₹1,28,756. ₹75,000 adds 6% simple growth each year and understates the effect of compounding inflation."], ["Retirement Corpus & Distribution Planning", "A client needs ₹10 lakh in the first year of retirement, rising 6% a year, for 20 years, withdrawn at the start of each year. If the corpus earns 8%, the required corpus is closest to:", ["₹2.00 crore", "₹1.06 crore", "₹1.43 crore", "₹1.68 crore"], 3, "PV of a growing annuity due = 10 lakh × Σ (1.06/1.08)^t for t = 0 to 19 ≈ ₹1.68 crore. ₹2.00 crore ignores returns and ₹1.06 crore ignores inflation in withdrawals."], ["Retirement Corpus & Distribution Planning", "Compared with an annuity, an SWP-based retirement income plan offers:", ["A guaranteed income for life", "Flexibility and access to capital", "Protection from all market risk", "Payments that never change"], 1, "An SWP keeps capital accessible and can be adjusted, but income depends on market returns and the corpus can run out. Annuities give guaranteed lifelong income but lock in capital."], ["Income Tax Fundamentals", "Advance tax is payable by an individual when:", ["He is a senior citizen with only pension income", "He files his return after the due date", "His income is entirely agricultural", "Estimated tax for the year exceeds a set threshold"], 3, "Advance tax must be paid in instalments during the year if estimated liability exceeds the prescribed limit. Resident senior citizens without business income are exempt."], ["Retirement Products & Pension Schemes", "An immediate annuity bought with a lump sum at retirement:", ["Defers income for at least ten years", "Pays only on death of the annuitant", "Starts regular income straight away", "Allows withdrawal of capital at any time"], 2, "An immediate annuity converts a lump sum into regular income starting soon after purchase. A deferred annuity starts payments later, and annuities usually do not allow free capital withdrawal."], ["Comprehensive Financial Planning & Case Studies", "A financial plan's recommendations are presented to the client. What should follow?", ["Locking the plan with no changes ever", "Selling all existing investments at once", "Implementation and periodic monitoring", "Sending the plan only to the bank"], 2, "After agreement, the plan is implemented and regularly monitored and revised. Plans must adapt to changes; wholesale selling may cause unnecessary costs."], ["Life Insurance Products", "A money-back policy is best described as a policy that:", ["Pays only if the insured survives to 100", "Pays part of the sum assured at intervals", "Returns all premiums if no claim is made", "Pays the sum assured only on accidental death"], 1, "A money-back policy pays survival benefits periodically during the term, with full sum assured payable on death. It is a variant of endowment assurance."], ["Insurance Needs Analysis", "A homemaker has no income. Why may she still need life insurance?", ["Insurers do not offer cover to homemakers", "Her family would gain financially from her death", "Her services would cost money to replace", "Life cover is mandatory for all adults"], 2, "Childcare, home management and other services have economic value that the family would need to pay for. Cover can be sized on this replacement cost."], ["Wills, Succession & Trusts", "Under the Indian Trusts Act, the person who holds and manages trust property for others is the:", ["Settlor", "Beneficiary", "Trustee", "Executor"], 2, "The trustee holds legal title and manages property for beneficiaries as per the trust deed. The settlor creates the trust and the beneficiary enjoys the benefit."], ["Income Tax Fundamentals", "An individual is resident in India for a tax year if, among other conditions, he is in India for at least:", ["30 days in that year", "365 days in that year", "182 days in that year", "90 days in each of the last ten years"], 2, "Staying in India for 182 days or more in the year makes an individual resident. There is also an alternative 60-day plus 365-day test, with modifications for certain citizens."], ["Retirement Products & Pension Schemes", "Employer contributions to EPF are split between EPF and the Employees' Pension Scheme. The EPS portion provides:", ["A monthly pension after retirement", "A lump sum gratuity on resignation", "Health cover for the family", "Life insurance of a fixed amount"], 0, "Part of the employer's contribution goes to EPS, which pays a monthly pension based on pensionable salary and service. EDLI provides insurance; gratuity is separate."], ["Taxation of Investment Products", "On sale of a residential house, what is the holding period after which the gain is long-term?", ["More than 12 months", "More than 24 months", "More than 36 months", "More than 60 months"], 1, "Immovable property such as a house is a long-term capital asset if held for more than 24 months. Listed shares need more than 12 months."], ["Life Insurance Products", "A participating (with-profit) policy differs from a non-participating policy because it:", ["Offers guaranteed returns linked to the stock market", "Shares in the insurer's surplus via bonuses", "Has no surrender value at any time", "Cannot be bought by salaried individuals"], 1, "Participating policies receive bonuses from the insurer's surplus, which are not guaranteed in advance. Non-participating policies offer only contracted benefits."], ["Wealth Management & Estate Planning Concepts", "An executor of a will is responsible for:", ["Writing the will on behalf of the testator", "Challenging the will in court", "Carrying out the will's instructions", "Collecting income tax from the heirs"], 2, "The executor administers the estate after death: collecting assets, paying debts and distributing property as per the will, and may apply for probate where needed."], ["Wills, Succession & Trusts", "A Hindu male dies intestate leaving his widow, two sons and his mother. Under the Hindu Succession Act, his self-acquired property is shared:", ["Only between the two sons", "Half to the widow and half to the sons", "Equally among all four", "Entirely to the mother"], 2, "Widow, sons and mother are all Class I heirs and take equal shares, so each gets one-fourth. Daughters, if any, would also share equally."], ["Insurance Needs Analysis", "A family's monthly expenses are ₹40,000 today. At 6% inflation, what will the same expenses cost per month in 20 years?", ["About ₹1,28,285", "About ₹88,000", "About ₹2,00,000", "About ₹64,000"], 0, "Future expense = 40,000 × 1.06^20 ≈ ₹1,28,285. ₹88,000 assumes simple growth of 6% a year. Inflation compounds, which is why needs analysis must project costs."], ["Life Insurance Products", "Which rider would pay an additional sum if the insured dies in an accident?", ["Waiver of premium rider", "Critical illness rider", "Hospital cash rider", "Accidental death benefit rider"], 3, "An accidental death benefit rider pays an extra amount if death is caused by an accident. A waiver of premium rider stops future premiums on specified events."], ["Risk Management & Insurance Concepts", "A client installs smoke detectors and fire extinguishers in his shop. This is an example of:", ["Risk transfer", "Risk retention", "Risk avoidance", "Risk reduction"], 3, "Taking steps to lower the likelihood or severity of a loss is risk reduction or loss control. It complements insurance but does not transfer the risk to an insurer."], ["Taxation of Investment Products", "Short-term capital gains on listed equity shares sold after 23 July 2024 are taxed at:", ["15%", "Slab rate", "20%", "10%"], 2, "The Finance (No. 2) Act, 2024 raised the STCG rate on listed equity and equity-oriented funds, where STT is paid, from 15% to 20% for transfers on or after 23 July 2024."], ["Wills, Succession & Trusts", "A testator wants to make a small change to his existing will without rewriting it. He can do so through a:", ["Probate", "Codicil", "Succession certificate", "Trust deed"], 1, "A codicil is a document that alters, explains or adds to a will and must be executed like a will. Probate and succession certificates are court documents."], ["Health & General Insurance", "Portability in health insurance allows a policyholder to:", ["Switch insurer while keeping waiting-period credit", "Transfer the policy to a friend", "Convert health cover into life cover", "Claim from two insurers for the same bill"], 0, "Portability lets a policyholder move to another insurer without losing credit for waiting periods already served. It does not allow transfer to another person or double recovery."], ["Retirement Products & Pension Schemes", "A retirement-oriented mutual fund scheme under SEBI's solution-oriented category has a lock-in of:", ["No lock-in", "Five years or till retirement, whichever is earlier", "One year", "Until age 75"], 1, "SEBI's retirement funds are solution-oriented schemes with a lock-in of at least five years or until retirement age, whichever is earlier, encouraging long-term saving."], ["Comprehensive Financial Planning & Case Studies", "A married couple has a child. Which change should trigger an immediate plan review?", ["A routine rise in the index", "The birth of the child", "A change in the bank's branch timings", "A new mobile phone"], 1, "A new dependant changes insurance needs, cash flows, goals and estate plans. Routine market moves do not by themselves require a full plan review."], ["Health & General Insurance", "A critical illness policy differs from a hospitalisation policy because it:", ["Reimburses actual hospital bills only", "Covers only outpatient consultation", "Pays only if the insured dies", "Pays a lump sum on diagnosis"], 3, "Critical illness cover is a benefit policy paying a fixed sum on diagnosis of a listed illness, regardless of actual bills. Hospitalisation policies are indemnity covers."], ["Wills, Succession & Trusts", "A trust in which the trustee has discretion over how much each beneficiary receives is a:", ["Discretionary trust", "Specific trust", "Public charitable trust", "Revocable will"], 0, "In a discretionary trust, beneficiaries' shares are not fixed and the trustee decides distributions. In a specific (determinate) trust, shares are fixed in the deed."], ["Wealth Management & Estate Planning Concepts", "Estate planning for a client with a child having special needs often uses:", ["A trust to manage assets for the child", "Only a joint bank account with the child", "A nomination in favour of a minor sibling", "No planning, as courts will decide"], 0, "A trust can provide professional management and ongoing support for a dependent who cannot manage money, with clear instructions on use of funds."], ["Life Insurance Products", "A policyholder transfers the rights under his life policy to a bank as security for a loan. This is called:", ["Assignment", "Nomination", "Surrender", "Revival"], 0, "Assignment transfers the policy's rights and benefits to another person or entity, such as a lender. A nominee only receives the claim money; ownership remains with the policyholder."], ["Retirement Products & Pension Schemes", "Which scheme is aimed mainly at workers in the unorganised sector, offering a guaranteed minimum pension?", ["Employees' Provident Fund", "Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme", "Atal Pension Yojana", "Pradhan Mantri Vaya Vandana Yojana"], 2, "APY offers a guaranteed minimum monthly pension after 60 depending on contribution, targeting unorganised sector workers. EPF is for organised sector employees and SCSS is a deposit scheme for seniors."], ["Taxation of Investment Products", "A resident sells listed equity shares held for two years with a long-term gain of ₹2,00,000 in FY 2025-26, with no other gains. Ignoring cess, the tax is:", ["₹25,000", "₹20,000", "₹12,500", "₹9,375"], 3, "Long-term gains on listed equity above ₹1,25,000 are taxed at 12.5%: (2,00,000 − 1,25,000) × 12.5% = ₹9,375. ₹25,000 wrongly taxes the whole gain at 12.5%."], ["Retirement Corpus & Distribution Planning", "In a bucket strategy, the long-term bucket is usually invested in:", ["Growth assets such as equity", "Savings bank account balances", "Overnight funds only", "Cash kept at home"], 0, "The long-term bucket is meant for expenses many years ahead, so it can hold growth assets that beat inflation. Near-term buckets hold safe, liquid assets."], ["Retirement Planning Concepts", "Which action would most reduce a client's required retirement corpus, all else equal?", ["Retiring earlier", "Assuming higher inflation", "Planning for a longer life expectancy", "Retiring later"], 3, "Retiring later shortens the retirement period and lengthens the saving period, reducing the corpus needed. Earlier retirement, higher inflation and longer life all raise it."], ["Taxation of Investment Products", "Sovereign gold bonds held until maturity give investors:", ["Exemption on redemption gains", "Taxable gains at 30% on redemption", "Tax-free interest every year", "A deduction for the purchase cost"], 0, "Capital gains on redemption of SGBs at maturity are exempt for individuals. The annual interest is taxable at slab rates, and there is no deduction on purchase."], ["Comprehensive Financial Planning & Case Studies", "A client has two goals competing for limited surplus: a car in two years and retirement in 25 years. A sensible approach is to:", ["Fund retirement and size the car goal to fit", "Ignore retirement until the car is bought", "Take a large loan for both goals", "Put all savings into the car goal"], 0, "Long-term needs like retirement benefit from early saving, so they should not be neglected. The discretionary goal can be scaled down or delayed to fit the budget."], ["Retirement Products & Pension Schemes", "Under NPS, the subscriber's contributions are invested by:", ["The subscriber's employer directly", "Banks as fixed deposits only", "The Employees' Provident Fund Organisation", "PFRDA-registered pension fund managers"], 3, "NPS contributions are managed by pension fund managers regulated by PFRDA, across equity, corporate bonds, government securities and alternative assets as chosen by the subscriber."], ["Risk Management & Insurance Concepts", "A death occurred because an accident led to an injury that caused fatal infection. The principle used to decide whether the accident cover applies is:", ["Proximate cause", "Indemnity", "Contribution", "Subrogation"], 0, "Proximate cause identifies the dominant, effective cause in an unbroken chain of events. If the accident set the chain in motion, the accident cover typically responds."], ["Taxation of Investment Products", "Units of a debt mutual fund bought in 2024 are sold after three years at a gain. How is the gain taxed?", ["At 12.5% as long-term gains", "At 20% with indexation", "Fully exempt after three years", "At slab rates, irrespective of holding period"], 3, "For specified funds with up to 35% in equity, units bought on or after 1 April 2023 have gains taxed at slab rates regardless of holding period, with no indexation."], ["Health & General Insurance", "A health policy has a room-rent limit of 1% of sum insured per day. Choosing a costlier room usually leads to:", ["The insurer paying the full bill anyway", "Proportionate deduction from related charges", "Automatic cancellation of the policy", "A refund of the premium paid"], 1, "With room-rent caps, insurers often reduce associated charges proportionately when a costlier room is taken, so the claim payout falls more than the room-rent difference."], ["Insurance Needs Analysis", "Which approach to life insurance needs looks at the economic value of a person's future earnings?", ["Need-based approach", "Rule of 72", "Expense ratio method", "Human life value approach"], 3, "HLV estimates the present value of future income the person would contribute to the family. The need-based approach instead sums specific future needs and liabilities."], ["Retirement Products & Pension Schemes", "The Voluntary Provident Fund allows a salaried employee to:", ["Contribute more than the mandatory EPF share", "Withdraw EPF money every month", "Invest EPF money in equity directly", "Transfer EPF to his spouse's account"], 0, "VPF lets employees contribute above the statutory EPF contribution, earning the EPF interest rate. The employer is not required to match the extra contribution."], ["Health & General Insurance", "Which motor insurance cover pays for damage to the insured's own vehicle as well as third-party liability?", ["Third-party liability only policy", "Personal accident cover for the owner-driver", "Engine protection add-on alone", "A comprehensive (package) policy"], 3, "A comprehensive policy combines own-damage cover with mandatory third-party liability. A standalone third-party policy does not pay for damage to one's own vehicle."], ["Health & General Insurance", "A no-claim bonus in health insurance usually results in:", ["A cash refund of all premiums paid", "Higher sum insured after claim-free years", "A lower deductible for life", "Removal of all waiting periods"], 1, "NCB rewards claim-free years, typically by increasing the sum insured (cumulative bonus) or lowering premium. It is not a refund of premiums."], ["Income Tax Fundamentals", "Health and education cess on income tax is levied on:", ["Gross total income", "Only salary income", "Only capital gains", "Tax plus surcharge"], 3, "Cess of 4% is charged on the amount of income tax plus any surcharge, not on income directly. It applies across heads of income."], ["Life Insurance Products", "Under the free-look provision, a new life insurance policyholder can:", ["Get a full refund at any time during the term", "Change the insurer without any formalities", "Return the policy within the free-look period", "Increase cover without medical tests"], 2, "The free-look period lets a new policyholder review terms and cancel the policy for a refund, after deductions for proportionate risk premium and expenses. IRDAI has extended this period to 30 days."], ["Wealth Management & Estate Planning Concepts", "Why is keeping an updated list of assets, accounts and policies important in estate planning?", ["It helps heirs trace and claim assets", "It reduces the income tax rate payable", "It replaces the need for a will", "It is required to open a demat account"], 0, "Heirs often struggle to locate assets, leading to unclaimed deposits and policies. A clear record speeds up transmission and reduces disputes."]]};
